        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink">1. Uematsu-Franck Relative Permittivity Tuning</h3>
        <p>
          At ambient temperatures (25°C), water is an extremely polar solvent (<span class="font-mono text-ink">ε_r ≈ 78.5</span>), preventing dissolution of moderately hydrophobic polyphenols and aglycones. Under subcritical hydrothermal conditions (110–190°C, 25–60 bar), thermal agitation disrupts hydrogen bonding networks, dropping the dielectric constant:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl font-mono text-[11px] text-cyan-300">
          ε_r(T, ρ) = 1 + (A_1 / T*)·ρ* + (A_2 / T* + A_3 + A_4·T*)·ρ*² + ...
        </div>
        <p>
          At 160°C–180°C, <span class="font-mono text-ink">ε_r drops to 35–39</span>, exactly matching the dielectric polarity of methanol (<span class="font-mono text-ink">ε_r = 32.7</span>) and ethanol (<span class="font-mono text-ink">ε_r = 24.3</span>). This empowers pure water to rapidly solubilize high-value proanthocyanidins and polymethoxyflavones with zero toxic solvent footprints.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink">2. Multi-Stage Counter-Current Mass Transfer Kinetics</h3>
        <p>
          Operating in a continuous counter-current column maintains maximum concentration driving forces <span class="font-mono text-ink">(C* - C)</span> across all N stages. According to the Kremser-Brown-Souders equation for solid-liquid extraction:
        </p>
        <div class="p-3 bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl font-mono text-[11px] text-teal-300">
          N = ln[ ( (X_in - Y_in/m) / (X_out - Y_in/m) ) · (1 - 1/E) + 1/E ] / ln(E)
        </div>
        <p>
          Where <span class="font-mono text-ink">E = m·(L/S)</span> represents the extraction factor. Counter-current staging slashes water consumption down to 4.0–7.0 L/kg (compared to 25–50 L/kg in batch tanks), significantly reducing thermal energy loads and preventing solute thermal degradation.
        </p>
